//@version=6
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  BODY INSIDE THE PREVIOUS UPPER WICK
//
//  Marks a bar whose OPEN and CLOSE both sit inside the band the previous bar
//  spiked into and gave back:
//
//        prevHigh  ┬  ← the spike
//                  │     ← the band: everything above the previous body
//        prevTop   ┴  ← max(open[1], close[1])
//
//  The previous bar went up there and refused to close there. This bar opens
//  AND closes inside that refused area — the zone was rejected, then accepted.
//
//  WHY BOTH ENDS, NOT THE WHOLE BAR
//  Open and close are where the auction actually settled; high and low are where
//  it merely visited. Requiring the whole range inside the band would demand the
//  bar never poke out even for a second, which is a much rarer and different
//  claim than "it traded and closed in there".
//
//  THE BAND MUST BE A BAND
//  A previous bar with a one-tick upper wick gives a band a few cents wide, and
//  almost any small body lands inside it by arithmetic rather than by meaning.
//  minWickPct sets how much of the previous bar's range the wick has to be
//  before the question is worth asking at all.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  TWO OUTCOMES FROM THE SAME START, and they cannot both happen
//
//    orange  opened in the band and CLOSED IN IT     — the refused zone accepted
//    blue    opened in the band and CLOSED ABOVE      — the refused zone taken out
//    red     opened in the band and CLOSED BELOW      — the refused zone refused again
//
//  All three begin with the open inside the band, and differ only in where the
//  close landed. They exclude each other by arithmetic, not by a priority rule
//  that could drift — and together they cover EVERY bar that opened in the band,
//  so nothing falls between them.
//
//  Measured on 774,129 sp500 bars: 120,446 opened in the band (15.6% of all
//  bars) and resolved held 27.3% · broke 32.8% · fell 39.9%. The most common
//  outcome is the red one, which is why leaving it out was worth fixing —
//  a chart showing only two of three reads as if the third never happened.
//
//  TWO MORE, STARTING FROM BELOW THE BAND
//
//    green   opened inside the previous BODY, closed INSIDE the upper wick band
//            — rose into the rejected zone without clearing it
//    navy    opened inside the previous BODY, closed ABOVE the whole band
//            — crossed the rejected zone end to end in one bar
//
//  The orange case starts inside the band and stays; these two arrive from
//  underneath and differ only in whether they cleared it. Green is to navy what
//  orange is to blue — the same pair of outcomes, one starting lower.
//
//  Nothing here can overlap: the open is either inside the body or inside the
//  band, never both, and the close is either under prevHigh or over it.
//
//  AND THE LOWER WICK, one case of it
//
//    purple  opened AND closed inside the previous LOWER wick band
//            [low[1] … min(open[1], close[1])]
//
//  The mirror of orange: the previous bar spiked down, refused to close down
//  there, and this bar settles inside that refused area. Only this one case is
//  drawn, because only this one was asked for — its two siblings (closing below
//  the old low, or back above the old body) are the same three-way split turned
//  upside down and are deliberately absent rather than forgotten.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
indicator("Previous wick zones — held, broken, or reclaimed", overlay = true, max_labels_count = 500)

minWickPct = input.float(20.0, "Previous upper wick ≥ % of its bar's range",
             minval = 0, maxval = 90, step = 5,
             tooltip = "How big the previous bar's upper wick must be before the band counts. At 0 a one-tick wick qualifies and the signal becomes arithmetic.")
strict     = input.bool(false, "Require the WHOLE bar inside (high and low too)",
             tooltip = "Applies to the ORANGE case only. Off: open and close inside — where the auction settled (4.25% of bars). On: the entire range inside, the bar never left the band at all (0.12% — 35× rarer). NOTE: with this on the three outcomes no longer cover every bar that opened in the band, because a bar that closed inside but poked outside becomes unmarked.")

showHeld   = input.bool(true,  "🟠 held — closed inside the UPPER band")
showBroke  = input.bool(true,  "🔵 broke — closed above the band")
showFell   = input.bool(true,  "🔴 fell — closed below the UPPER band")
showSurge  = input.bool(true,  "🔷 surged — opened in the previous BODY, closed ABOVE the band",
             tooltip = "The full version of the green case: from inside the previous body, through the whole rejected zone, closing above the previous high. Green and this differ only in whether the band was cleared.")
showRise   = input.bool(true,  "🟢 rose — opened in the previous BODY, closed in the upper band",
             tooltip = "Arrives at the same place as the orange case but from underneath: the open sits inside the previous bar's body [min(open[1],close[1]) … max(open[1],close[1])] and the close lands in the upper wick band without exceeding it. A previous bar with no body makes this impossible rather than trivial — the open would have to match one exact price — so it needs no separate guard.")
showLower  = input.bool(true,  "🟣 opened and closed inside the LOWER wick band",
             tooltip = "The mirror of the orange case, on the previous bar's lower wick: [low[1] … min(open[1], close[1])]. Uses the same minimum-wick threshold, measured against the lower wick.")
showNear   = input.bool(true,  "× mark bars stopped ONLY by the band-size rule",
             tooltip = "A bar that satisfies its case in every respect except that the previous bar's wick was too small to count as a band. Without this the rejection is silent, and a silent rejection reads as the script being broken — which is how this option came to exist.")
showBand   = input.bool(true,  "Draw the band")
showLabels = input.bool(true,  "Label with where the close landed")

// ── the previous bar's upper wick ────────────────────────────────────────────
prevTop   = math.max(open[1], close[1])       // top of the previous body
prevHigh  = high[1]
prevRange = high[1] - low[1]
wick      = prevHigh - prevTop

hasBand   = prevRange > 0 and wick > 0 and wick >= prevRange * (minWickPct / 100.0)

// ── and the previous bar's LOWER wick ────────────────────────────────────────
prevBot   = math.min(open[1], close[1])       // bottom of the previous body
prevLow   = low[1]
loWick    = prevBot - prevLow
hasLoBand = prevRange > 0 and loWick > 0 and loWick >= prevRange * (minWickPct / 100.0)

// ── this bar against it ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
openIn  = open >= prevTop and open <= prevHigh          // all three start here
bodyIn  = openIn and close >= prevTop and close <= prevHigh
wholeIn = bodyIn and low >= prevTop and high <= prevHigh

// The three outcomes, then the display switches applied on top. Kept separate so
// turning a colour off hides it without changing what the other two mean.
heldRaw  = hasBand and (strict ? wholeIn : bodyIn)      // closed inside
brokeRaw = hasBand and openIn and close > prevHigh      // closed above
fellRaw  = hasBand and openIn and close < prevTop       // closed below

// opened in the previous BODY, closed in the upper wick band. Mutually exclusive
// with every upper-band case above by construction: those all require the open
// inside the band, this one requires it below.
openInBody = open >= prevBot and open <= prevTop
riseRaw  = hasBand and openInBody and close >= prevTop and close <= prevHigh
surgeRaw = hasBand and openInBody and close > prevHigh

// the lower-wick mirror of heldRaw. `strict` is not applied here: it is an
// option on the orange case and silently extending it would change a setting's
// meaning without saying so.
loHeldRaw = hasLoBand and open >= prevLow and open <= prevBot
                      and close >= prevLow and close <= prevBot

rise   = riseRaw   and showRise
surge  = surgeRaw  and showSurge
held   = heldRaw   and showHeld
broke  = brokeRaw  and showBroke
fell   = fellRaw   and showFell
loHeld = loHeldRaw and showLower

// ── stopped only by the band rule ────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every case above requires hasBand. When that is the ONLY failing condition the
// bar is not "no pattern" — it is the pattern over a previous bar that barely had
// a wick, i.e. a zone too thin to be crossed or held. Worth seeing, because the
// fix is then a judgement about the threshold rather than about the bar.
wickPct   = prevRange > 0 ? wick / prevRange * 100 : 0.0
wouldFire = openIn or (open >= prevBot and open <= prevTop and close >= prevTop)
nearBand  = showNear and not hasBand and wick > 0 and wouldFire

plotshape(nearBand, title = "blocked by band size", style = shape.xcross,
          location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.gray, 45),
          size = size.tiny)

if showLabels and nearBand and barstate.isconfirmed
    label.new(bar_index, low, yloc = yloc.belowbar,
      text = "wick only " + str.tostring(wickPct, "#.#") + "% of the previous bar" + "\n(needs >=" + str.tostring(minWickPct, "#") + "%)",
      style = label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.gray, 30),
      textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)

// ── output ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// One line, deliberately. Pine rejects a continuation line that STARTS with an
// operator — "Mismatched input, expecting ':'" — and I wrote a comment about its
// fussy continuation rules in the other script and then broke this the same way.
bandCol = broke ? color.blue : surge ? color.navy : fell ? color.red : rise ? color.green : color.orange
if showBand and (held or broke or fell or rise or surge)
    box.new(bar_index - 1, prevHigh, bar_index, prevTop,
            border_color = color.new(bandCol, 40), bgcolor = color.new(bandCol, 88))

if showBand and loHeld
    box.new(bar_index - 1, prevBot, bar_index, prevLow,
            border_color = color.new(color.purple, 40),
            bgcolor = color.new(color.purple, 88))

plotshape(held, title = "held inside the wick", style = shape.triangledown,
          location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.orange, 0),
          size = size.small, text = "IN")

plotshape(broke, title = "closed above the wick", style = shape.triangleup,
          location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.blue, 0),
          size = size.small, text = "OUT")

plotshape(fell, title = "closed below the wick", style = shape.triangledown,
          location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 0),
          size = size.small, text = "DN")

plotshape(surge, title = "crossed the upper wick", style = shape.triangleup,
          location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.navy, 0),
          size = size.small, text = "SURGE")

plotshape(rise, title = "rose into the upper wick", style = shape.triangleup,
          location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.green, 0),
          size = size.small, text = "RISE")

plotshape(loHeld, title = "inside the lower wick", style = shape.triangleup,
          location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.purple, 0),
          size = size.small, text = "LO")

if showLabels and broke and barstate.isconfirmed
    // how far past the old high it closed, in units of the wick it just cleared —
    // "1.0×" means it added a second wick's worth on top of taking the first
    over = wick > 0 ? (close - prevHigh) / wick : na
    label.new(bar_index, low, yloc = yloc.belowbar,
      text = "OUT +" + str.tostring(over * 100, "#") + "% of the wick above it" +
             "\nwick was " + str.tostring(wick / prevRange * 100, "#") + "% of its bar",
      style = label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.blue, 25),
      textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)

if showLabels and held and barstate.isconfirmed
    // where in the band the body settled: 0% = sitting on the old body top,
    // 100% = right at the old high. High values mean the spike is being held.
    mid = (open + close) / 2
    pos = wick > 0 ? (mid - prevTop) / wick * 100 : na
    label.new(bar_index, high, yloc = yloc.abovebar,
      text = "IN " + str.tostring(pos, "#") + "% up the wick" +
             "\nwick was " + str.tostring(wick / prevRange * 100, "#") + "% of its bar",
      style = label.style_label_down, color = color.new(color.orange, 25),
      textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)

if showLabels and fell and barstate.isconfirmed
    // how far below the band it closed, in units of the wick it failed to hold
    under = wick > 0 ? (prevTop - close) / wick : na
    label.new(bar_index, high, yloc = yloc.abovebar,
      text = "DN −" + str.tostring(under * 100, "#") + "% of the wick below it" +
             "\nwick was " + str.tostring(wick / prevRange * 100, "#") + "% of its bar",
      style = label.style_label_down, color = color.new(color.red, 25),
      textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)

if showLabels and surge and barstate.isconfirmed
    // how far past the old high, in units of the wick it crossed — the same
    // measure the blue case uses, so the two read against each other
    sOver = wick > 0 ? (close - prevHigh) / wick * 100 : na
    sSpan = prevTop - prevBot
    sOpen = sSpan > 0 ? (open - prevBot) / sSpan * 100 : na
    label.new(bar_index, low, yloc = yloc.belowbar,
      text = "SURGE +" + str.tostring(sOver, "#") + "% of the wick above it" +
             "\nopened " + str.tostring(sOpen, "#") + "% up the body",
      style = label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.navy, 25),
      textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)

if showLabels and rise and barstate.isconfirmed
    // where the close landed in the band, and where the open started in the body
    rPos = wick > 0 ? (close - prevTop) / wick * 100 : na
    bSpan = prevTop - prevBot
    oPos = bSpan > 0 ? (open - prevBot) / bSpan * 100 : na
    label.new(bar_index, low, yloc = yloc.belowbar,
      text = "RISE closed " + str.tostring(rPos, "#") + "% up the wick" +
             "\nopened " + str.tostring(oPos, "#") + "% up the body",
      style = label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.green, 25),
      textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)

if showLabels and loHeld and barstate.isconfirmed
    // 0% = sitting on the old low, 100% = right at the old body bottom
    loMid = (open + close) / 2
    loPos = loWick > 0 ? (loMid - prevLow) / loWick * 100 : na
    label.new(bar_index, low, yloc = yloc.belowbar,
      text = "LO " + str.tostring(loPos, "#") + "% up the lower wick" +
             "\nwick was " + str.tostring(loWick / prevRange * 100, "#") + "% of its bar",
      style = label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.purple, 25),
      textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)

// A bar can satisfy an upper-band case and the lower-band case only if both wicks
// are large and the body is tiny — the upper cases win the bar colour so the
// three-way split above stays readable, and the 🟣 marker still prints.
barCol = broke ? color.blue : surge ? color.navy : fell ? color.red : held ? color.orange : rise ? color.green : loHeld ? color.purple : na
barcolor(na(barCol) ? na : color.new(barCol, 20))

alertcondition(held, "Held inside the previous upper wick",
     "{{ticker}} — opened and closed inside the previous bar's upper wick at {{close}}")
alertcondition(broke, "Closed above the previous upper wick",
     "{{ticker}} — opened in the previous upper wick and closed above it at {{close}}")
alertcondition(fell, "Closed below the previous upper wick",
     "{{ticker}} — opened in the previous upper wick and closed back below it at {{close}}")
alertcondition(surge, "Crossed the previous upper wick",
     "{{ticker}} — opened in the previous body and closed above its high at {{close}}")
alertcondition(rise, "Rose into the previous upper wick",
     "{{ticker}} — opened in the previous body and closed inside its upper wick at {{close}}")
alertcondition(loHeld, "Inside the previous lower wick",
     "{{ticker}} — opened and closed inside the previous bar's lower wick at {{close}}")
